From 5bc3faa3145770d3e42761851cd5997f68560c33 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: SorinS <ssuciu@gmail.com>
Date: Sat, 21 Nov 2026 23:33:04 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] -g: a natvis beside the map, embedded in the PDB by an MSVC
 link

On Windows the debuggers that read a PDB -- Visual Studio, VS Code's
cppvsdbg, WinDbg and cdb -- show values through natvis, static XML
matched on C type names. -g now writes one beside the debug map,
<stem>.natvis, and goose build's MSVC link stores it in the PDB
(/link /NATVIS:), from where those debuggers load it with nothing to
configure: cdb's .nvlist names it as the exe's embedded NatVis.

- natvis.h writes it from the map and the C: an entry for each slice,
  fixed and limited array type the C's typedefs declare (natvis has no
  wildcard for sl_*), each struct and fixed-mode enum under its Goose
  field, variant and tag names, each variant's payload type, and the
  globals block. bool, u8, T&, T?, relative references and resizable
  fields are shown by their Goose types, which the C type cannot tell
  apart; a struct with nullable or resizable fields has one summary per
  combination of their states, up to 48.
- It is written on every platform, as the map is: where the C is
  written the C compiler is not yet known (goose -g -o prog.c names
  none, and goose build finds it after), and a C file may be compiled
  elsewhere. /NATVIS goes only to an msvc-style link (cl, clang-cl).
- json.h's reader reads the map back.

Test: on seven sample programs, `goose build -g` with MSVC 19.44 puts
the natvis in the PDB, and cdb 10.0.26100 with nothing loaded shows
every Goose local and global as goose-debugger's gen_natvis.py --all
does, but for the guessed [as T] views of a resizable local; --debug
alone writes and embeds none. The compiler suite passes on macOS and
on Windows.
